review.statement_review:
The theorem is quantified over every $c:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ and has no further hypotheses. It asserts that the images in \leanname{Gm} of the following are all $1$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $X_{-1}(0)$ and $Y_{-1}(0)$;
\item $X_{\ell,jk}(0)$ for every $e\in E$;
\item $Y_{\ell,jk}(0)$ for every $e\in E$.
\end{itemize}
These are exactly parts (a) through (d).

To recover the source statement, instantiate $c$ with the coefficients of $J$ restricted to $j\in\mathbb{N}$; these are natural numbers. The value $c(-1)$ never enters $E$.

The conclusion is not vacuous. Parts (a) and (b) are unconditional, and parts (c) and (d) range over a nonempty $E$ for the true $c$. The relation set also has no degenerate encoding.

review.proof_review:
\begin{itemize}
\item \leanname{add\_zero\_group\_lemma} shows that if $f(u+v)=f(u)f(v)$ in a group, then $f(0)=1$. It does this by deriving $f(0)=f(0)f(0)$ and cancelling, which is exactly the source argument with $u=v=0$.
\item \leanname{mk\_xm\_add}, \leanname{mk\_ym\_add}, \leanname{mk\_xx\_add} and \leanname{mk\_yy\_add} turn the relations (Re:1a), (Re:1b), (Im:1a) and (Im:1b), each a member of the relation set, into multiplicativity of the image in \leanname{Gm}. They use \leanname{PresentedGroup.one\_of\_mem} or \leanname{PresentedGroup.mk\_eq\_mk\_of\_mul\_inv\_mem}.
\item \leanname{solution} combines these four lemmas with \leanname{add\_zero\_group\_lemma}.
\end{itemize}
The proof follows the source proof, and its note covering parts (b) to (d). No auxiliary lemma is weaker or stronger than the step it represents.
